    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Spatial continuity and linkage of faults may substantially affect flow either by compartmentalizing the reservoir, or by increasing the tortuosity of flow pathways whether the faults act as seals or conduits. Therefore, understanding fault linkage geometry should improve reservoir flow simulation models and in turn significantly reduce the number of wells required to drain reserves.\r\n\r\nA method has been developed to infer, in three dimensions, the fault tip-line geometry below the seismic resolution as well as potential fault linkage using 3D seismic data and elastic dislocation based analysis.\r\n\r\nA 3D model of the faulted reservoir and its surroundings is built from seismic interpretation. Such a model, combined with an appropriate set of boundary conditions is used to compute the fault slip distribution as well as vertical displacement field. By comparing intrpreted fault slip distribution to computed slip distribution adjacent to potential intersection lines, the geomechanical models can constrain the geometry of the faults as well as the location of the intersection line between faults. The interpreted structure contour map and theoretical displacement field also are compared to constrain the fault geometry.\r\n\r\nTwo subsurface examples from the Oseberg Syd reservoir, northern North Sea, have been used to illustrate how geomechanical analysis can increase confidence in a seismic interpretation to refine fault connectivity and reservoir compartmentalization.",
